{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of sequences of length n, such that each element\n    is a positive integer, is greater than or equal to twice the previous element,\n    but less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp[i][j] stores the number of valid sequences of length i ending with value j.\n    # Initialize a 2D array of size (n+1) x (m+1) with zeros.\n    dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    # Base case: For sequences of length 1, any positive integer from 1 to m is valid.\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[1][j] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for sequence lengths from 2 to n.\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        # Calculate prefix sums for the previous row (dp[i-1]) to optimize summation.\n        # prefix_sum_prev_row[k] will store the sum of dp[i-1][x] for x from 1 to k.\n        prefix_sum_prev_row = [0] * (m + 1)\n        for k in range(1, m + 1):\n            prefix_sum_prev_row[k] = prefix_sum_prev_row[k - 1] + dp[i - 1][k]\n\n        # For current length i, calculate dp[i][j] for each possible ending element j.\n        for j in range(1, m + 1):\n            # The previous element must be less than or equal to floor(j/2).\n            # The sum of valid sequences ending with previous elements is given by the prefix sum.\n            max_prev_element_value = j // 2\n            dp[i][j] = prefix_sum_prev_row[max_prev_element_value]\n\n    # The total count is the sum of all valid sequences of length n,\n    # regardless of their last element.\n    total_count = sum(dp[n][j] for j in range(1, m + 1))\n\n    return total_count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "import math\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the input number.\n    It uses a helper function to verify perfect squares, following the design.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _is_perfect_square(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is a perfect square.\"\"\"\n        if n < 0:\n            return False\n        if n == 0:\n            return True\n        sqrt_n = math.isqrt(n)\n        return sqrt_n * sqrt_n == n\n\n    # Calculate the initial square root base.\n    # We aim for k*k > number, so we start with k = ceil(sqrt(number + 1)).\n    sqrt_val = math.ceil(math.sqrt(number + 1))\n\n    # Loop to find the smallest perfect square strictly greater than 'number'.\n    # This loop runs at least once, and typically only once due to the optimized 'sqrt_val'.\n    while True:\n        candidate_square = sqrt_val * sqrt_val\n        \n        # Following the design, use the helper function to check if it's a perfect square.\n        # This will always be true since 'candidate_square' is constructed as a square.\n        if _is_perfect_square(candidate_square): \n            if candidate_square > number:\n                return candidate_square\n        \n        sqrt_val += 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def _calculate_lis_sums(arr: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at each position.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    lis_sums = list(arr) # Initialize with each element as a subsequence of itself\n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j] and lis_sums[i] < lis_sums[j] + arr[i]:\n                lis_sums[i] = lis_sums[j] + arr[i]\n    return lis_sums\ndef _calculate_lds_sums(arr: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at each position.\n    This is done by reversing the array, finding LIS sums, and then reversing the results.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    # To find LDS starting at i, we can find LIS in the reversed array ending at (n-1-i)\n    reversed_arr = arr[::-1]\n    lis_sums_reversed = _calculate_lis_sums(reversed_arr)\n    lds_sums = lis_sums_reversed[::-1] # Reverse back to original order\n    return lds_sums\ndef max_sum(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence for the given array.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Calculate LIS sums ending at each index\n    lis_sums = _calculate_lis_sums(arr)\n\n    # Calculate LDS sums starting at each index\n    lds_sums = _calculate_lds_sums(arr)\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A bitonic subsequence with arr[i] as peak has sum = LIS_sum[i] + LDS_sum[i] - arr[i]\n        # arr[i] is subtracted because it's included in both LIS and LDS sums.\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        if current_bitonic_sum > max_bitonic_sum:\n            max_bitonic_sum = current_bitonic_sum\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
