{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(list1: list[float], list2: list[float], size: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the median of two sorted lists of the same size.\n\n    Args:\n        list1: The first sorted list of numerical values.\n        list2: The second sorted list of numerical values.\n        size: The common number of elements in both lists.\n\n    Returns:\n        The calculated median as a floating-point number.\n    \"\"\"\n    i = 0  # Pointer for list1\n    j = 0  # Pointer for list2\n    m1 = -1.0 # Stores the (size)-th smallest element found\n    m2 = -1.0 # Stores the (size+1)-th smallest element found\n\n    # Iterate 'size + 1' times to find the size-th and (size+1)-th smallest elements\n    for count in range(size + 1):\n        # Case 1: Both lists still have elements\n        if i < size and j < size:\n            if list1[i] < list2[j]:\n                m1 = m2\n                m2 = list1[i]\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                m1 = m2\n                m2 = list2[j]\n                j += 1\n        # Case 2: list2 is exhausted, take from list1\n        elif i < size:\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        # Case 3: list1 is exhausted, take from list2\n        else: # j < size (since size is positive, one must still have elements until count reaches size+1)\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n            \n    # The median is the average of the size-th (m1) and (size+1)-th (m2) elements\n    return (m1 + m2) / 2.0"}
